.TH JIGGLE 1
.SH NAME
jiggle \- keep the computer on the USB cable awake (mouse jiggler)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBjiggle\fR [\fB\-i\fR \fIseconds\fR] [\fB\-p tiny\fR|\fBzero\fR] &
\fBjiggle \-k\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Jiggle
writes a mouse report to
.B /dev/hidmouse
every
.I seconds
(default 30, 1 to 3600), so that the computer the Flipper is plugged into
does not lock its screen or go to sleep (Galtzagorriak MINIX, spec
R11.3.6).
Start it in the background; the Jiggler page of Teklatua does the same.
.TP
.B \-p tiny
(the default) moves the pointer one pixel right, down, left and up: it
ends where it was.
.TP
.B \-p zero
sends a report that does not move it at all.
.TP
.B \-k
stops the running
.BR jiggle .
.PP
.B Jiggle
sleeps in
.BR poll ()
between reports.
While the cable is out, the computer has not set the Flipper up yet, has
suspended the USB bus, or another USB persona (the canary, a reader mode)
holds it, it sends nothing and does not wake at all until that changes.
.PP
While it runs, the desktop shows
.B J
on the status line
.RB ( "guimod status jiggle J" ).
Only one
.B jiggle
runs at a time.
It takes about 1 KB of RAM (its text runs from flash).
.SH FILES
.TP 20
.B /dev/hidmouse
the USB mouse (mode 0660, group usb)
.TP
.B /tmp/jiggle
the FIFO
.B "jiggle \-k"
writes into (the running one's, mode 0600)
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 stopped by
.BR "jiggle \-k" ,
1 an error (no access to
.BR /dev/hidmouse ,
already running; for
.BR \-k :
not running), 2 usage.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR guimod (1).
